          ],
          evolution: "While Bisca required following suit or trumping in its Italian/Portuguese origins, Albastini dropped this constraint, allowing for deeper 'sacrifice' plays.",
          cultural: "Commonly played in 'vijiwe' (social meeting points) in Tanzania. Winning a trick is called 'Kula' (eating).",
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        setup: {
          players: '2, 3, or teams of 2 (4 players) or 3 (6 players).',
          deck: '36-card standard (Remove 2s, 8s, 9s, 10s).',
          equipment: 'Standard modified deck, score sheet.',
          dealing: '5 cards dealt to each player. Remaining cards form the stock. Dealer is chosen by agreement.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Capture tricks containing high-value cards to surpass 60 points (out of 120 total).',
          gameplay: "1. **Bidding (Otea)**: Dealer's opponents place one card face-up. This signifies their desire for that suit to be trumps. 2. **Trump Flip**: Dealer turns up the top card of the stock. This suit becomes trump. If an opponent bid that suit, they swap their bid card for the trump card. 3. **The Play**: Lead any card. Others play any card (no follow-suit requirement). 4. **Winning**: Highest trump wins. If no trump, highest card of the led suit wins. 5. **Drawing**: Winner 'eats' the trick, draws a new card from the stock first, then others draw. Lead next. 6. **Endings**: Play continues until stock and hands are empty.",
          keyRules: [
            'Card Values: Ace=11, 7=10, King=4, Jack=3, Queen=2. Total in deck = 120.',
            'Ndoa (Marriage): When a 7 is captured by an Ace of the same suit.',
            'Mrithi (Target): To avoid a double-loss, you must reach a minimum point threshold (10 for individuals, 20 for 4-player teams, 30 for 6-player teams).',
            'No follow-suit requirement: You can play any card at any time.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Don't waste your trumps early. Use low-value cards (6, 5, 4, 3) to probe your opponents and safe-guard your points.",
          intermediate: "Strategic Passing: Since you don't have to follow suit, use your point-cards (Ace/7) to win tricks that contain high-value discards from your partner.",
          advanced: "Card Counting: There are only 36 cards. If you track the fallen 10s and face cards, you can calculate precisely when it's safe to drop your 7s.",
          tips: [
            "The '7' is the second highest card in Albastini—it is extremely valuable and should be protected/hunted.",
            'Always try to win the second-to-last trick to draw the face-up trump card.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        variations: {
          list: [
